{"answer":"No. The algebraic argument is a valid proof by contradiction, but its claimed conclusion has the direction reversed. Assuming sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms gives a^2=2b^2. Since an even square has an even base, a is even; writing a=2k then gives b^2=2k^2, so b is even as well. Thus 2 divides both a and b, contradicting lowest terms. Consequently no lowest-terms integer quotient can equal sqrt(2), and sqrt(2) is irrational (using the standard fact that a rational number has such a representation).","approaches":[{"description":"Assume a lowest-terms representation and derive that both numerator and denominator are divisible by 2.","id":"contradiction-route","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It establishes a contradiction with the assumption, hence refutes rather than proves the assumed rationality."},{"description":"Read the displayed equations as direct evidence that sqrt(2) is rational.","id":"affirm-rationality","outcome":"rejected","reason":"The equations arise only under the rationality assumption, and their consequence contradicts the lowest-terms condition."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question states: \"write sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms, square to get a^2=2b^2\".","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"assumed-representation","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["derive-even-numerator","derive-even-denominator","lowest-terms-contradiction"],"statement":"For contradiction, assume sqrt(2)=a/b for integers a,b with b nonzero and with a/b in lowest terms; then a^2=2b^2.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the parity argument in derive-even-numerator.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"numerator-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the assumed representation, a is even.","step_ids":["derive-even-numerator"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","b