er r, so a^2=4r(r+1)+1 would be odd. Hence a is even.","statement":"Establish that a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["derive-even-numerator"],"id":"derive-even-denominator","justification":"Write a=2k. Substitution into a^2=2b^2 gives 4k^2=2b^2, hence b^2=2k^2. The same odd-square argument used in derive-even-numerator shows that b is even.","statement":"Establish that b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["derive-even-numerator","derive-even-denominator"],"id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","justification":"The preceding steps give 2|a and 2|b. Thus a and b have the common divisor 2, contrary to the scoped lowest-terms assumption.","statement":"Derive the contradiction with lowest terms."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"id":"infer-irrationality","justification":"Apply the lowest-terms representation theorem: if sqrt(2) were rational, it would admit the representation assumed in assumed-representation. But lowest-terms-contradiction rules out every such representation.","statement":"Conclude that sqrt(2) is irrational."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-contradiction","infer-irrationality"],"id":"assess-logical-direction","justification":"A proof by contradiction assumes a proposition and derives an impossibility; it establishes the proposition's negation. Here the assumed proposition is rationality, so the argument cannot establish rationality.","statement":"Assess the direction of the stated proof."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
